    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm looking for some help with a load calculation. im planning to add a small loft to a residential build here in oregon using douglas fir. the span for the main header is gonna be about 14 feet. i'm trying to figure out what size beam i need to account for a decent live load so i dont end up with too much deflection. can u walk me through what i should be looking at for the sizing and the load distribution?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To size the beam for a 14-foot span in Oregon, you must first multiply the estimated floor live load (typically 40 psf for residential lofts) by the tributary area to get the total concentrated load on the header, then add dead loads from the Douglas Fir framing and roofing materials. Using an engineering calculator like RaftalqNT or RDX with your specific species (Douglas Fir-Larch No. 1 or No. 2), you'll need to check bending stress, deflection limits (usually L/360 for/live load), and shear capacity rather than relying on a single remote-applied point formula. Finally, you should consult the specific Oregon Soil Classification map for the slope of your site, as this determines whether you are designing for standard vertical loads or the additional uplift forces required for a High Risk or Slope Critical region. Always have a licensed structural engineer review these inputs and final specifications to ensure local code compliance before procurement.",
