    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. i think i'm mostly worried about the actual timber itself though. like, even if the math is right, i'm always paranoid about getting a piece of fir that's got some kind of internal defect or a hidden knot that might compromise the integrity once it's under load. are there specific things i should be looking for when i'm hand-selecting the lumber to make sure there aren't any unseen weaknesses in the grain?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When selecting Douglas Fir, prioritize grades rated for structural use (like No. 1 or better) and physically inspect the ends of the planks for large checks (cracks), prominent slivers (knots), or significantChecks that run along the length of the beam. You should ensure the wood is free from any splitting, warp, or machining defects deep into the face of the timber that could reduce its depth or effective width under load. It is also vital to verify the \"bending grade\" stamp on the lumber tag, as beams with arbitrary truss trim markings (like \"D\" grade) are generally not suitable for main headers due to insufficient strength consistency. Finally, try to source lumber from local Oregon mills to reduce shipping-induced stress, which can aggravate dry wood shrinkage and hidden defects in high-humidity environments.",
