    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Structurally, you cannot definitively detect internal defects like internal splits or low-density \"burn\" zones without cutting into the beam or using expensive non-destructive testing methods like ground-penetrating radar or dimensional scanning, which are rarely practical for on-site framing. While you cannot guarantee a perfect beam visually, structurally engineered lumber is graded identically regardless of whether you cut it into a header or a floor joist, so the strength data remains consistent. The best insurance against hidden failures is to rely on a specialist inspector or engineer to verify the source and ensure the wood is handled correctly by mill finishers before shipment. Ultimately, hiring a licensed structural engineer is your only true safeguard against unexpected failures, as they can balance the beam selection with connection details and hardware to withstand real-world variations.",
